20:49:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a very fair analogy to bring up, and it lands well—reputations *can* be grounded in real, repeated experiences rather than prejudice. I don’t dismiss that at all. Let’s run with your used car salesmen example, because it actually helps pinpoint something important. The reason people say "used car salesmen are shady" isn't that there's something about a person's DNA that makes them dishonest when they sell pre-owned vehicles. It’s that the *structure of the market*—information asymmetry (they know about the car, you don’t), high-pressure commissions, one-time transactions with few repeat customers—creates strong incentives for opportunistic behavior. Enough people get burned, and a reputation forms that’s about the role, not the individual. Now, apply that lens to Afghan business deals falling through more often. The question becomes: what’s the structural equivalent of the used car lot? - Decades of war that destroyed institutions and normal contract enforcement - A cash-based economy where written agreements are often less powerful than personal relationships—and if that relationship sours, you have almost no legal recourse - Extreme uncertainty where even a well-intentioned partner may overpromise because they genuinely believe they can deliver, until reality intervenes - Middlemen who emerge precisely because formal channels are broken, sometimes creating layers where accountability disappears In that environment, even a fundamentally honest person may end up in a transaction that goes bad. And yes, the environment also attracts or rewards those willing to cut corners—just as a poorly regulated used car market does. The result is a real, elevated risk of failed deals. Your trade journal data reflects that. Where I’d gently push back is on the leap from “the deal failure rate is high” to “Afghan people are inherently not trustworthy.” The first is an environmental risk you can mitigate; the second is a character judgment that may